comments.body:
💡 **SUGGESTION: cancellation classification rides a cancel-reason type heuristic — the run token itself is available and never wired**

```dart
bool get _runCancelled {
  if (cancelToken?.isCancelled ?? false) return true;
  final source = budgetSource;
  if (source == null) return false;
  return source.token.isCancelled &&
      source.token.cancelReason is! TimeoutException;
}
```

"User abort" is inferred by ruling out `TimeoutException` reasons on the budget token. That holds
for today's callers (I checked: `_activeRun?.source.cancel()` passes no reason, steering cancels
pass strings), but it is a coupling, not a contract: a future caller that cancels the run token
with a `TimeoutException` reason (a watchdog, for example) is silently misclassified as a budget
kill — the escalation then proceeds, its LM call dies instantly, and the abort dissolves into a
"summarizer returned no text" failed receipt: exactly the #1085 shape this PR set out to avoid.

The robust signal already exists and is one line at the factory: `_runStructured` holds `runToken`
(the factory links it into `budgetSource` right there) and `StructuredCompactor` already has a
`cancelToken` constructor field that production never populates:

```dart
final compactor = StructuredCompactor(
  // ...
  cancelToken: runToken, // authoritative user-abort signal for _runCancelled
  budgetSource: budgetSource,
);
```

With that, the first clause of `_runCancelled` becomes the primary check and the reason-type
heuristic degrades to a harmless fallback. Please consider wiring it (and adding the IT-level
abort test from the coverage comment so the link stays proven).

# Thread 2 — coverage for the escalation's "no foldable range" branch (+ adjacent paths)

All three flagged paths now have pinned tests in `test/compaction/structured_escalation_test.dart`:

1. **The `range == null` branch (gh-2044 semantic 5)** — new group `UT-ESC-12 — the keep-recent boundary cannot fold the whole live world (gh-2044 rework)`, test *"an escalation whose foldable path fits keepRecentTokens with live records left declines: no fold, no live-tail swallow, honest no-range receipt, verdict stands"* (line ~1003). It pins the fixture this branch is reachable through and asserts exactly what the thread asked:
   - exactly ONE `checkpoint-escalation` pass, `ok == false`, error `contains('no foldable range')`;
   - no additional `CompactCheckpointRecord` appended;
   - `run()` still returns `true` (verdict stands);
   - `summarizeCalls == 0` — no LM call may summarize the live tail away;
   - the live user ask and tail filler survive in the projection.
   This test doubles as the regression test for the CI failure it exposed (core shards 1/4 + 3/4: `subagent produced no assistant message` on resumed child runs — the whole-world fold swallowed the live turn).

2. **The `hideableRemaining = 0` diagnostic branch** — new test *"the hideableRemaining diagnostic reads 0 when every entry is exempt or inside the protected tail"* (line ~1105): `protectLastN: 16` puts every visible non-exempt record inside the protected tail, and the escalation receipt is asserted to carry `hideableRemaining == 0`. The literal `view == null ? 0` arm inside `_runCheckpointEscalationPass` stays defensive-only and is not directly driven: an over-trigger settle requires a non-empty projection, so `_buildView()` cannot return null on a path that reaches the escalation; driving it would mean calling the private pass with a synthetic state rather than through `run()`. Flagging that honestly rather than faking coverage.

3. **The factory-level abort wiring** — new `IT-ESC-6: a user abort mid-escalation through the factory surfaces — even when the cancel reason is a TimeoutException` (line ~1229): goes through `AutoCompactorFactory.run()` with the production `runToken → budgetSource` link, cancels mid-escalation with a watchdog-shaped `TimeoutException` reason, and asserts `throwsA(isA<CancelledException>())`, exactly one entry into the escalation stream, and **no** failed `structured·ckpt-escalation` receipt masking the abort.

All green locally: `dart test test/compaction/` → 442 passed; the two `test/task/` suites that were red in CI pass as well.

# Thread 3 — wire the factory's `runToken` into the engine's `cancelToken`

Done — exactly the one-line wiring suggested:

```dart
// lib/src/agent/auto_compactor.dart (AutoCompactorFactory._runStructured)
final compactor = StructuredCompactor(
  // ...
  // gh-2044 rework (review thread 3): the factory's run token is the
  // AUTHORITATIVE user-abort signal for the engine's `_runCancelled`. ...
  cancelToken: runToken,
  budgetSource: budgetSource,
);
```

(`lib/src/agent/auto_compactor.dart:1177`)

With that, `_runCancelled` (`lib/src/compaction/structured/engine.dart:442`) decides from the first clause — `cancelToken?.isCancelled` — and the reason-type heuristic on the budget token degrades to a fallback for callers that only pass `budgetSource`. A watchdog-shaped `TimeoutException` reason can no longer reclassify a user abort as a budget kill, so the #1085 shape (abort dissolving into a "summarizer returned no text" failed receipt while the run reports success) is closed at the source.

The budget-expiry semantics stay as specified: per-call attempt-budget kills cancel the budget token with a `TimeoutException` reason (issue #515) and must NOT suppress the escalation (gh-2044 AC1) — covered by *"an attempt-budget expiry during the escalation propagates as the named TimeoutException"*; and the factory's total-budget race now also latches a dedicated `_totalBudgetSource` so no attempt starts and nothing appends/reports after that authoritative outcome (`UT-ESC-5b`).

Proven end-to-end by the IT-level test this thread requested: `IT-ESC-6: a user abort mid-escalation through the factory surfaces — even when the cancel reason is a TimeoutException` — `AutoCompactorFactory.run()` + watchdog-shaped cancel reason mid-escalation → `throwsA(isA<CancelledException>())`, escalation entered exactly once, no masking failed-escalation receipt.

# Thread 4 — `judgeCalls` under-reports chunked escalation folds

Took the second option: the escalation pass now reports the **real** summarizer call count.

- `_summarizeRange` returns `({String? text, int summarizerCalls})` (`lib/src/compaction/structured/engine.dart:1057`):
  - marker-only deterministic consolidation → `summarizerCalls: 0` (line 1086, issue #387 — no LLM call, none reported);
  - single in-window fold → `summarizerCalls: 1` (line 1177);
- `_chunkedFold` counts its per-chunk calls and returns the real N, including the mid-fold failure path (`text: null, summarizerCalls: calls`, lines 1184–1221);
- the escalation receipt emits `judgeCalls: visibleMessages == 0 ? 0 : summarizerCalls` (line 862) — 0 for the marker-only path, 1 for a single fold, N for a #729 chunked fold — so the deepening's observability matches its actual work.
- The classic checkpoint ladder keeps its historical `judgeCalls: 1` receipt shape unchanged (documented in-place at the call site, line ~677) to avoid churning every existing receipt consumer in one PR; the escalation pass — whose whole purpose is observability of a previously invisible pass — is where the real count starts, as suggested.

Tests pinning the counts in `test/compaction/structured_escalation_test.dart`:
- *"a range needing several chunked summarizer calls reports exactly N summarizer calls folded into ONE escalation pass record"* — multi-chunk range → one `checkpoint-escalation` record with the real N;
- *"a marker-only escalation range consolidates deterministically"* — `judgeCalls == 0`, no summarizer invocation;
- single-fold escalation receipts assert the count of 1.
